- How would you test starvation scenarios when many tenants drop CKNE to near-zero?
- How would you handle new tenants with no historical CKNE data?

## Why This Is Asked

Realistic API gateway policy is needed to manage multi-tenant CKNE dynamics at the edge. It tests your ability to translate CKNE signals into safe routing and concrete SLA guarantees, with observable metrics.

## Key Concepts

- CKNE-driven token buckets per tenant
- Minute-wise rebalancing and fairness guarantees
- WASM/Envoy or Istio plugin implementation
- Telemetry for latency, throughput, and token usage

## Code Example

```javascript
// Simple token bucket with CKNE scaling (conceptual)
class TokenBucket {
  constructor(rate, burst) {
    this.rate = rate;
    this.burst = burst;
    this.tokens = burst;
    this.last = Date.now();
  }
  tick() {
    const now = Date.now();
    const dt = (now - this.last) / 1000;
    this.last = now;
    this.tokens = Math.min(this.burst, this.tokens + dt * this.rate);
  }
  tryConsume(n = 1) {
    this.tick();
    if (this.tokens >= n) { this.tokens -= n; return true; }
    return false;
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you test starvation scenarios and correctness under CKNE drift?
- How would you adapt the policy for multi-region deployments and clock skew?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design multi-hop, CKNE-aware backpressure and fairness across thousands of tenants with SLA-based tuning.

## Key Concepts
- CKNE health as a control signal for fan-out
- Per-tenant token bucket and dynamic scaling
- SLA-tier influences on limits and queuing
- Edge backpressure signaling and aggregation
- Payload schemas and end-to-end test plan

## Code Example
```javascript
function computeLimit(health, queueLen, sla) {
  const base = 1000;
  if (health > 0.8 && queueLen < sla.maxQueue * 0.7) return Math.min(base * 5, sla.maxCap);
  if (health < 0.4 || queueLen > sla.maxQueue * 0.9) return Math.max(base * 0.2, sla.minCap);
  return base;
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Edge compute with CKNE requires concrete scheduling strategies, policy definitions, and measurable SLAs under dynamic workloads.

## Key Concepts
- CKNE-to-budget mapping: slots, memory, and concurrency limits per tenant
- Minute-by-minute scheduler: preemption rules, reallocation, and SLA guarantees
- Edge runtimes and observability: lightweight workers, per-tenant state, and metrics

## Code Example
```javascript
// Minimal pseudo-scheduler snippet
function decideSlotAllocation(state, ckne) {
  // ckne in [0,1], returns slot budget for tenant
  // higher ckne => more slots
  const base = state.baseSlots;
  const ramp = Math.max(0.0, Math.min(1.0, ckne));
  return Math.floor(base * (0.5 + ramp * 0.5));
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you detect and mitigate starvation under bursty CKNE changes?
- What metrics would you expose for fairness and SLA adherence?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design CKNE-driven incentives in streaming, including how CKNE affects throughput, partitioning, and latency, plus a stable rebalancing loop and end-to-end validation.

## Key Concepts
- CKNE-to-throughput/partition mapping
- Kafka partition rebalancing and windowing impact
- Baseline SLA guarantees with dynamic prioritization
- Telemetry, monitoring, and stability guarantees

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo-mapping for CKNE-driven resource allocation
function mapCKNETokens(ckne, baseThroughput, basePartitions, kappa, lambda, minP, maxP){
  const T = baseThroughput * (1 + kappa * (ckne - 0.5));
  const P = Math.max(minP, Math.min(maxP, Math.round(basePartitions * (1 + lambda * (ckne - 0.5)))));
  return {throughput: T, partitions: P};
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to translate a health metric into concrete runtime constraints in a multi-tenant ML infra scenario.

## Key Concepts
- CKNE-to-resource mappings
- Cache budgeting, TTLs, prefetch strategies
- Fairness guarantees and SLA preservation
- Telemetry: per-tenant hit rate, latency, CKNE trend

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo-degradation loop sketch
function degradeLoop(state){
  const totalBudget = state.totalCacheMB;
  const sumCKNE = state.tenants.reduce((s,t)=>s+t.ckne,0);
  state.tenants.forEach(t=>{
    t.cacheMB = totalBudget * (t.ckne / (sumCKNE||1));
  });
}
```

